<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Importance of Water – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Use Water Everyday</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Interactive: Discuss personal future plans. Productive: Develop simple arguments based on texts studied.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss personal future plans to help save water (e.g., "I will use clean water for cooking.")<br>Can develop simple arguments about the importance of water conservation (e.g., "Water is an important resource in Panama. If we protect our rivers and streams, we will have fresh water for a longer time.")<br>Can express ideas about water-related projects in class discussions (e.g., "Our ideas to save water are: 1. Turn off the tap. 2. Collect rainwater.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 45%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 45%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 10%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a short video (1 minute) with different scenes of people using water daily (e.g., brushing teeth, washing dishes, cooking, showering, watering plants). After the video, the teacher asks students: "What did you see? How do people use water every day?" to elicit simple responses and activate prior knowledge related to the theme "How We Use Water Everyday." Students share orally in small groups, then a few share with the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target grammar: "We use water for drinking and cooking." and "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone." The teacher writes these on the board. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of stress patterns for compound words like "forecast" and "important" by saying them aloud and clapping on the stressed syllable. "FORE-cast, im-POR-tant."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Do we use water only for drinking?" (Expected answer: "No, for cooking, cleaning, too.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What happens if we don't save water?" (Expected answer: "We will have water shortage / less water.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two friends discussing their daily water usage and ideas for conservation, presented on a slide or handout. The dialogue incorporates the first conditional, "for + -ing," and "because" clauses, along with key vocabulary (e.g., <i>save, waste, tap, potable, resource, shortage, collect rainwater</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Hey, Carlos! How do you use water every day?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Well, I use water for drinking, cooking, and showering. It's an essential resource! If we don't save water, we will face water shortages."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "That's true. I try to turn off the tap while brushing my teeth. We save water because it is a limited resource."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students recognize and understand everyday expressions and basic phrases related to water usage and conservation. They can identify the main ideas about water's importance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher plays the dialogue (or reads it aloud, or students read it silently/aloud in pairs).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are Ana and Carlos talking about?" (Expected: "How they use water and how to save it.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students decide if statements are True or False based on the dialogue (e.g., "Carlos uses water for cleaning his car." False. "Ana turns off the tap." True).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practice using the First conditional, "for + verb + ing" to express purpose, and "Subject + verb + complement + because + cause" when discussing water usage and conservation. Use vocabulary: <i>save, waste, turn off, collect, potable, limited, important, resource, shortage</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences orally, then share with a partner. The teacher provides sentence beginnings on the board or a worksheet. Example: "We use water for ____." (Expected: "drinking," "cooking," "cleaning"). "If we ____, we will ____." (Expected: "If we collect rainwater, we will have more water.") "We conserve water because ____." (Expected: "it is an important resource.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Chain Drill (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student starts a sentence using a target structure, and the next student continues it or creates a new one, incorporating a new vocabulary word or conditional clause. E.g., Student 1: "If we waste water..." Student 2: "...we will have a shortage." Student 3: "We must not waste water because it's a limited resource." The teacher guides with prompts if needed. Emphasize clear pronunciation of words like "important" and "forecast" if they come up.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interview (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks Partner B questions about their daily water use and conservation plans, using prompts provided by the teacher. Example prompts: "What do you use water for at home?" "What will happen if we don't save water?" "Why do you think it's important to collect rainwater?" Students practice formulating answers using the target grammar. They listen to each other and provide feedback on clarity and use of vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion & Presentation of Solutions (Sociolinguistic: discuss and propose solutions to environmental issues). Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to prepare and present "Our Water Saving Plan."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups. Each group receives a scenario card (e.g., "Your community has a water shortage problem," "Your school wants to start a water conservation project").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss for 5-7 minutes their ideas to save water in their given scenario. They must use the target grammar (first conditional, "for + -ing," "because") and vocabulary (e.g., <i>tap, leak, conserve, filter, purify, rainwater, drop, important, limited</i>) to formulate arguments and solutions. Example discussion points: "If we fix the tap, we will not waste water." "We could collect rainwater for watering plants because it saves potable water."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then appoints a spokesperson to present their "Water Saving Plan" to the class (1-2 minutes per group). The presenter should clearly explain their ideas and arguments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other groups listen and can ask one clarifying question after each presentation, promoting interactive discussion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and presentations. Focus on individual and group participation in speaking activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group discussion and present their group's water-saving plan to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., clear articulation of words, correct stress patterns for compound words like "forecast", "important").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., <i>save, waste, tap, resource, shortage, collect rainwater, potable</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> (Ideas are presented logically; use of first conditional, "for + -ing," and "because" clauses).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> (Active participation in group discussion, respectful interaction).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving class.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about saving water today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to explain why water is important (choose one) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's discussion did I enjoy the most, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I will try to do this week to save water at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group discussions were very engaging, and most students successfully used the first conditional. Some students struggled with consistently using "for + verb + ing" correctly for purpose; more practice may be needed. Pronunciation of "resource" and "shortage" improved after explicit modeling. For the next lesson, I will ensure specific sentence frames are available for less confident speakers to build their writing from spoken practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about water conservation plans and arguments. In Lesson 4, students will use these ideas and structures to practice writing compositions about water conservation, building on the arguments they developed today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>